@.str.s10191 = private unnamed_addr constant [3432 x i8] c"\0A    Usage:\0A\0A    Project commands -- these read build.ums, found at or above the\0A    working directory, and write to .prismio/build/<profile>/:\0A      prismio init [name]                        start a project here, or in a new directory\0A      prismio build [--release] [target...]      build every target, or the ones named\0A      prismio run [--release] [target] [-- args...]\0A                                                 build, then run the executable target\0A      prismio test [--release] [test...]         build and run the test targets\0A      prismio clean [--release]                  remove this profile's build output\0A      prismio <name> [args...]                   run a command the manifest declares\0A\0A    Single-file commands -- these name a source and need no manifest:\0A      prismio build <source.psm> [-o output] [-g] [-O0..-O3|-Os|-Oz] [--overflow-checks]\0A                    [--target <triple>] [--sysroot <sdk>]\0A                    [--freestanding [--native <file>]... [--native-flag <flag>]...\0A                     [--link-arg <arg>]...\0A                     [--target-features <list>] [--code-model <model>] [--no-red-zone]]\0A      prismio run <source.psm> [-o output] [-g] [--overflow-checks] [--jit]\0A                    [-- args...]\0A      prismio check <source.psm> [--diagnostic-format=json] [--partial]\0A                    [--overlay <file.psm> <text>]... [--module <name>]\0A                    [--emit-index <file> [--complete <file.psm> <line> <column>]]\0A      prismio fmt <source.psm>... [--check] [--write] [--range <from>:<to>]\0A                                   format source, or check that it is formatted\0A      prismio lsp                  the language server, over standard input and output\0A\0A      prismio --version            which compiler, LLVM and standard library\0A      prismio --help-all           every command and flag, the compiler-development ones too\0A\0A          The debug profile builds with -g and --overflow-checks; --release\0A          builds optimised with neither. A manifest can change either in a\0A          `profiles` block.\0A\0A          -g emits DWARF, so the program can be run under lldb or gdb, and\0A          drops the object step to -O0: a variable folded into a register is a\0A          variable a debugger cannot show you.\0A\0A          -O0..-O3 set how much the program is optimised: -O3 without -g, -O0\0A          with it, when none is given. -Os and -Oz optimise for size; -Oz also\0A          outlines repeated machine code, smaller and usually slower.\0A\0A          --freestanding builds for a machine with no operating system: the\0A          installed runtime is not linked, no C library or startup files are,\0A          and the image is static. `main` is the function your entry stub calls.\0A          --native adds a C or assembly source the toolchain's clang compiles\0A          and links (the boot stub), --native-flag passes one flag to that\0A          compile (-mgeneral-regs-only), --link-arg passes one argument to the\0A          linker (-T kernel.ld), and --target-features, --code-model and\0A          --no-red-zone set what a kernel must (see docs/FREESTANDING_PLAN.md).\0A          The program provides every runtime symbol it uses.\0A\0A          --target takes an LLVM target triple -- x86_64-apple-macos,\0A          aarch64-linux-android, x86_64-pc-windows-msvc,\0A          x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, wasm32-unknown-unknown. --sysroot points\0A          clang at that target's SDK.\0A    \00"
